"# Concurrency Analysis Report\n\n**History:** T1: R(A), W(A), R(B), W(B), Commit · T2: R(A), W(A), R(B), W(B), Commit · T3: R(A), R(B), Commit\n**Assumed mechanism:** Snapshot Isolation with MVCC versioning and first-committer-wins (FCW) commit validation.\n\n## Isolation Level\n\n**Snapshot Isolation.** The behavioral evidence confirms consistency with SI and rules out weaker levels: T2 and T3 read only committed versions (`v0` — no dirty reads), every transaction reads from one fixed snapshot (no non-repeatable reads or read skew), and the write-write conflict between T1 and T2 is resolved by **FCW aborting T2** at commit validation. Under Read Committed or last-writer-wins MVCC, both T1 and T2 would have committed, silently losing T1's updates. Note: the *committed projection* of this history is serializable, but that is a property of this particular access pattern, not a guarantee of the isolation level (SI still permits write skew in general).\n\n## Conflicts and Graph\n\n| Pair | Conflicts | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 ↔ T2 | rw (bidirectional, A and B) + ww on A, B | ww conflict → **FCW aborts T2** |\n| T1 ↔ T3 | T1 --rw--> T3 (T3 read v0, T1 wrote v1) | No cycle — T3 read-only |\n| T2 ↔ T3 | Moot — T2 aborted, excluded from committed graph | — |\n\n**Committed MVSG:** nodes {T0, T1, T3}; edges `T0 --wr--> T1`, `T0 --wr--> T3`, `T1 --rw--> T3`. **Acyclic.** (Counterfactual without FCW: `T1 ⇄ T2` mutual-rw 2-cycle → cyclic, non-serializable.)\n\n## Anomalies\n\n| Anomaly | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| **Lost update** | ⚠️ Threatened — T1 and T2 both read-modify-write A and B from the same `v0` basis; **prevented** by FCW aborting T2 |\n| Dirty read | Absent — only committed versions visible |\n| Non-repeatable read / read skew | Absent — single fixed snapshot per transaction |\n| Write skew | Absent — write sets overlap {A,B} ∩ {A,B} ≠ ∅ → plain ww conflict, which FCW catches |\n| Phantom | Not applicable — no predicate/range operations |\n\n## Serialization Result\n\n**Serializable.** T2's abort removes all edges touching it; the committed projection {T1, T3} is conflict-equivalent to the serial schedule **T3 → T1** (both read `v0` from T0, T1 writes `v1`, final state A=v1, B=v1). **T1 → T3** is equally valid if T3 snapshots after T1 commits — T3 is read-only and can always be placed at its snapshot point.\n\n**Bottom line:** Snapshot Isolation accepts this history as serializable at the cost of aborting T2. The serialization outcome here comes from the FCW abort, not from an isolation-level guarantee — a workload shaped differently (e.g., write skew with disjoint write sets) could still break serializability at this level."